 "deviations": [
  "As in 1.4.1, the instance role (multiomics-inputs-20261004-1940) cannot write under rapidglm/, so pikachu pulled checkpoints hourly (instance-side rsync under nice 19) and results over SSH and pushed them to AWS object storage; this time the relay (scripts/relay.sh) and the finisher (scripts/finisher.sh) ran detached on pikachu CPUs 0-1 under nice 19 from the start.",
  "The check panel is the 1.4.1 panel file (same pairs, SHA-256 344c5b38...), not a new selection.",
  "Pikachu performance cores (CPUs 0-15) are capped at 2.2 GHz since 2026-10-23 after hardware faults; the pikachu image build, container tests and check panel ran on CPUs 0-7 under nice, untimed. Every copied file is checked by SHA-256 at both ends.",
  "Coordinator limits: the pikachu 1.5.0 timed receipts ran at the same time under the shared timing lock on CPUs 16-31; this run never took the lock, used CPUs 0-7 for the image build and the check panel, and CPUs 0-1 with at most 2 threads otherwise.",
  "The image rapidglm:1.5.0 was built once and is shared with the pikachu 1.5.0 receipts run, whose runner checks its ID; it was not rebuilt or retagged.",
  "At 2026-10-25 08:04 UTC the relay's first hourly checkpoint check of the legacy run found no finished tile yet (empty hash list) and logged a spurious 'does not match'; nothing was due for copying, and every later hourly copy matched at the instance, pikachu and AWS object storage.",
  "User instruction relayed at about 14:10 UTC on 2026-10-25 ('I want pikachu free for the next few days for spatial demo work'): from 14:15 UTC the relay and the finisher ran on pikachu CPU 0 only, at nice 19, doing only copies and SHA-256 checks; finisher.sh was replaced by finisher_v2.sh, which runs the analysis on the instance before termination.",
  "The analysis (scripts/analyze.py: the full-table passes, the panel check and the comparison with 1.4.1) ran on the instance in the rapidglm:1.5.0 container (python3, numpy, scipy of the image). Its panel check read the instance's panel tables, which are byte-identical to pikachu's (check_panel). The 1.4.1 tables were fetched to the instance from AWS object storage with time-limited download links (the instance role cannot read under rapidglm/) and checked against the 1.4.1 SHA256SUMS and SHA256SUMS.tables.",
  "Pikachu keeps each mode's table as rapid.tsv.gz (checked against the instance's SHA256SUMS); on the instance each rapid.tsv was checked against SHA256SUMS.tables and its rapid.tsv.gz was checked to decompress to the same SHA-256. A preliminary legacy analysis on pikachu (CPUs 0-1, 12:55-13:20 UTC, before the instruction) found p-values of exactly 0 (3 pairs in each version, skew-normal tails beyond double range); the comparison leaves such pairs out of the correlations and counts them."
